<h1 class="article-title">Local Plan to Open Jaunliepāja Canal Waterfront Submitted for Public Consultation</h1>
<div class="block_text mt-5 mb-4">
<div class="row">
<div class="col"><div class="col-12 output"><p>A local development plan has been prepared for part of Jaunliepāja to open a currently closed canal waterfront to the public and establish conditions for balanced residential and public development. The draft plan was submitted for public consultation from 29 July to 6 September 2024 to collect residents’ views and institutional opinions.</p><p>The plan covers 10.77 hectares of Jaunliepāja beside the Trade Canal, between the Tram Bridge and the Old Cemetery and adjoining Kaiju, Jelgavas and Aldaru streets. Part of the area is currently within the restricted boundaries of the Port of Liepāja. The main proposed solutions focus on creating a diverse, sustainable public space with buildings, amenities and landscaping.</p><p>By restoring the historic street network, the plan would reopen links to the waterfront and provide access to the proposed pedestrian promenade from the Tram Bridge and Aldaru, Kroņu, Kapsētas and Jelgavas streets. Existing development would be organised into three new blocks partly reflecting the waterfront’s historic layout.</p><p>The local plan proposes changing the land-use designation from port technical infrastructure to mixed-use central development. Alongside preserving and renovating historically valuable buildings, this would allow new apartment buildings, offices, shops and service facilities, as well as tourism, leisure, sports and cultural facilities and related amenities. Mixed-use central development would be permitted in the block between the Trade Canal and Kaiju Street and on the opposite side of Kaiju Street.</p><p>“An open, publicly accessible Jaunliepāja waterfront will give our city not only a new appearance, but also new life and energy for Jaunliepāja. These changes will affect the daily lives of Liepāja residents, particularly those living in Jaunliepāja. I therefore invite people to take an active role in shaping their city by participating in the local plan consultation: examine the proposed solutions, join discussions and share their views,” said Liepāja Mayor Gunārs Ansiņš.</p><p>The Jaunliepāja canal waterfront is intended to become an important part of the city’s public space, with streets, squares, green corridors and a pedestrian promenade offering views of Old Liepāja. Planting will be a major landscape feature, while existing railway tracks and port infrastructure elements will be incorporated into the public space.</p><p>“Cargo ships are getting larger, and the Trade Canal has become too narrow and shallow for them. Cargo operations have gradually moved to other areas of the port: Winter Harbour, Freeport and Karosta. The berths on the Old Liepāja side of the Trade Canal are already publicly accessible, with fishing vessels and yachts mooring there and people enjoying walks. Restaurants, offices and hotels have moved into former port warehouses. We envision similar development on the Jaunliepāja side of the canal,” said Liepāja SEZ CEO Uldis Hmieļevskis.</p><p>The plan sets out detailed land-use and building regulations, including building zones, density and height limits. It also proposes infrastructure integrated with surrounding areas, including pedestrian and cycle routes, streets and utility networks.</p><p>Vehicle movements would be kept to a minimum, mainly to provide access to buildings. Only emergency and service vehicles would be allowed on the pedestrian promenade. The proposal would also connect two existing cycle-route sections between Aldaru Street and the Tram Bridge, helping create a continuous city cycling network.</p><p>Full details of the local plan for the area between Kaiju, Aldaru and Jelgavas streets and Jaunā ostmala can be found:</p><ul><li>on the national geospatial information portal www.geolatvija.lv, under Spatial Development Planning (https://geolatvija.lv/geo/tapis#document_25742);</li><li>on the municipal website www.liepaja.lv, under Public Participation – Public Consultations (https://www.liepaja.lv/aktualas-apspriesanas/);</li><li>in person in the ground-floor lobby at Peldu Street 5 during office hours.</li></ul><p>Two meetings will be held so that residents and institutions can review and assess the proposed amendments to the spatial plan:</p><ul><li>21 August at 18:00 – an in-person walk through the local planning area (meeting location to be confirmed);</li><li>28 August at 17:00 – an online public consultation meeting via Zoom.</li></ul><p>To help prepare the participant list, please register in advance and indicate which meeting you wish to attend by completing the form at epakalpojumi.liepaja.lv/lv/projects/preview/366/ or emailing your name, surname and represented sector to buvvalde@liepaja.lv.</p><p>Please register at least one day before the event (by 20 August for the site visit or 27 August for the Zoom meeting) to receive information about the consultation arrangements.</p><p>Comments and suggestions on the draft local plan are invited until 6 September 2024 inclusive:</p><ul><li>electronically via the national geospatial information portal https://geolatvija.lv/geo/tapis#document_25742, signing in through latvija.lv using online banking or an e-signature;</li><li>in person or by post to Liepāja Building Authority, Peldu Street 5, Liepāja, LV-3401, or by email to buvvalde@liepaja.lv. Individuals must provide their name, surname, residential address and telephone number; legal entities must provide their name, registration number, address and telephone number.</li></ul><p>For further questions and consultations, contact the Liepāja Building Authority by telephone on 63 404 725.</p><p>The local plan amending the Liepāja spatial plan for the area between Kaiju, Aldaru and Jelgavas streets and Jaunā ostmala up to O. Kalpaka Street has been prepared in accordance with the planning brief issued by Liepāja Building Authority and approved by Liepāja City Council at its meeting on 10 November 2022. It also incorporates residents’ opinions collected in a survey before the drafting process began.</p></div>
